# The PDF test fixtures and PNG golden reference images below are
# binary content, byte-exact by construction (see
# tools/genfixtures/main.go and pdfviewer_render_test.go). Without this
# file, Git falls back to each contributor's own core.autocrlf setting -
# on Windows (where Git for Windows defaults core.autocrlf to true),
# Git's text-vs-binary heuristic can misjudge a PDF or PNG as text and
# rewrite LF bytes to CRLF on checkout, corrupting a PDF's byte-exact
# cross-reference offsets or a PNG's compressed data stream. That
# corruption is what makes TestGeneratedFixturesMatchCheckedInFiles and
# TestRenderMatchesReferenceImages fail right after a fresh clone until
# `go run ./tools/genfixtures` (or `go test -update`) overwrites the
# corrupted files with correct bytes again - which then looks like a
# genuine content change to Git, tempting a Windows contributor to
# commit a CRLF-flavored version of the corpus.
#
# Marking these paths "binary" (Git shorthand for "-text -diff") makes
# Git never transform their bytes on checkout or commit, and never try
# to diff them as text, regardless of any contributor's autocrlf
# setting or platform. That is the actual fix: it does not matter
# whether the corpus was generated on macOS or Windows, because Git no
# longer touches the bytes either way.
testdata/fixtures/handmade/*.pdf binary
testdata/renderrefs/*.png binary

# The real-world fixture corpus (see testdata/fixtures/FIXTURES.md) needs
# the same protection for a stronger reason: these files are not
# regenerable at all. They are exactly what the software that produced
# them emitted, so a checkout that rewrote their line endings would be
# silently unrecoverable rather than merely stale.
testdata/fixtures/real-world/*.pdf binary
